WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.099 --> 00:00:07.640
BENEDICT CHONG: My
name is Ben Chung.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.500
I'm a Product Manager at Google.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.070
And as a product manager, I
need to set expectations for you

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.500
all, my customers, I guess.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.730
There's absolutely no
speaker notes here, nothing.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.030
So all the bad jokes
are all my fault, OK?

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:21.530
All right.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.196
So I'm going to talk
to you about moving

00:00:23.196 --> 00:00:24.340
your data to Cloud.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.680
It's actually easier
than you think.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.170
Hopefully, by the end of
this, you will believe so.

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:33.010
Oops.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.570
OK, I-- let me see.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.610
So how many of you knew that
this was Google's mission?

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.050
All right.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.310
About half, maybe.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:46.090
So our mission is to make all
the world's information easily

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.460
accessible and useful.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.710
The same infrastructure
that we are using

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.190
is now available to you
right through Google Cloud.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.340
We're basically using the same
platform, same applications,

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.690
making it available as part
of the Cloud platform product

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:03.190
suite.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.890
And it allows you to
make your company's data

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:14.550
available, accessible,
useful, getting value of that.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.370
So here's what you
can do with your data.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:21.710
You can run some analytics, run
some machine learning models

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:25.000
on it, create new services,
new products out of your data,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.530
or your existing data, or you
can move your applications

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:33.350
to the Cloud from data center,
and make it more usable,

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.300
serve content from Cloud.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.257
And also you can just store
data on Cloud for the long term.

00:01:38.257 --> 00:01:39.840
We have customers
trying to store data

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.150
or planning to store in
Cloud for next 99 years.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.090
But all these things
is possible only if you

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.860
can get all your data today.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:52.550
Most of the data in today's
world is in data center,

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.520
not in Cloud.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.770
And you realize
that it's actually

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.035
very hard to move data.

00:02:00.035 --> 00:02:01.410
And one reason
why you're here is

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.743
that you're thinking, that
is really difficult because I

00:02:03.743 --> 00:02:04.970
have a lot of data.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.070
I have very little bandwidth.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.110
There's a lot of
paperwork and red tape

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.700
I have to go through in order
to get network connectivity.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.270
How many of you remember seeing
as part of the intro slide

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:21.720
show the notable Evernote?

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.220
Yeah.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.678
So Evernote took
about seven months

00:02:23.678 --> 00:02:26.070
to move a few petabytes of data.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.370
They have a pretty large
team of people doing that.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.770
So they basically wrote
their own applications

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:32.720
with the GCS APIs.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.380
So it's kind of tough, even
with the kind of bandwidth

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.930
that they had.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:40.700
So how you get your
data to Google Cloud

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.980
depends on the kind
of data that you have

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.204
and the kind of data sources.

00:02:44.204 --> 00:02:45.620
The way we look
at the world today

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.590
is unstructured data,
which are files--

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:54.630
stuff on object or mass storage
devices in your data center

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:58.070
or structured data,
generally databases.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.350
So the stuff we're
going to talk about--

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.300
unstructured data.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.710
And for this I have my
colleague Ash who will cover it.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.634
ASH AHLUWALIA: Thanks, Ben.

00:03:10.634 --> 00:03:12.300
Thank you for joining
us this afternoon.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.880
My name is Ash
Ahluwalia, and I'm

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.190
a Product Manager that
works on large data

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:19.860
moves over the network,
primarily unstructured data.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.210
So one of the first questions,
or first couple of questions,

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:27.170
you'll need to answer when doing
a large unstructured data move

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.830
are one, how much data
are you looking to move?

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:34.340
And two, how much bandwidth
do you have to move it?

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:35.840
So just to give you
a sense of where

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.506
people land on this
spectrum in the room

00:03:37.506 --> 00:03:41.270
today, how many folks here
have a connection to the Google

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:44.630
Cloud that is faster than
10 gigabits per second?

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:48.250
Faster than 10 Gbps
by a show of hands.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.400
OK, a couple of folks.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.880
How many folks have
a connection speed

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.370
that is slower than one gigabit
per second to Google Cloud?

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.790
OK, a couple of other folks.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.570
How many people have no idea
how fast their connection is

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:03.720
to the Google Cloud?

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.020
OK, like 80% of you.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:07.970
So you now know one
of the questions

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:11.090
that you're going to have to go
answer after hearing this talk.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.030
So depending on where
you are on this spectrum,

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.060
your transfer can take anywhere
from seconds or minutes

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.250
to days, or even weeks.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.580
So it's important to understand
the answer to these two

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.740
questions before you start doing
a large unstructured data move.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.030
Now, this is a pretty
complicated graphic.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.830
So let me boil it
down a little bit.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:34.410
We can keep the y-axis as
how much data you're moving.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.010
And on the x-axis, instead
of bandwidth, let's

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.550
come up with an easier
concept to grasp.

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:44.190
You can think of this data by
how far away it is from GCP.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:47.990
So if you have data that is
sitting already inside of GCP,

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.782
or it's in another public
cloud vendor like AWS,

00:04:50.782 --> 00:04:52.490
there's pretty high
bandwidth connections

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:54.380
between those public
cloud vendors.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.300
And so even a moderately
large data set

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.170
can be moved pretty quickly.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.550
So in that sense, the data
is pretty close to GCP.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.284
Now, you can think
one step further away.

00:05:03.284 --> 00:05:04.700
Let's say you have
an on-prem data

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:09.201
center with a typical connection
speed to the public internet.

00:05:09.201 --> 00:05:11.450
And then you can even have
further afield data centers

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.320
that are in remote locations or
have limited or no connection

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:16.320
to the internet.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.349
In which case, even
a small data move

00:05:18.349 --> 00:05:19.640
is going to take a lot of time.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.060
In that sense,
it's far from GCP.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.780
So what we'll do today
is walk through products

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.760
on different ends
of this spectrum

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.780
and how you can use
them for your data move.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.800
So we'll start with data
that is close to GCP

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.210
with a product called the
Cloud Storage Transfer Service.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.080
So this product is
explicitly designed

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.100
to move data from public
cloud to GCP, whether that

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.740
is within GCP from
one region to another

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.659
or from another public
cloud like AWS to GCP.

00:05:48.659 --> 00:05:50.200
Some pretty common
use cases for this

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.870
include people that
are archiving or saving

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:56.800
a backup copy to GCS as part
of their multi cloud strategy.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.070
Some folks pull data
from other public clouds

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:03.850
into Google Cloud for analysis
in a tool like BigQuery.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:06.220
And then finally, some
folks use this tool

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.650
to move data between
GCS regions as part

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.020
of their daily operations.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.780
Now, in addition to pulling
data from S3 and GCS,

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:16.600
as I've talked about, the
Cloud Storage Transfer Service

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:21.850
can also pull data
from any data that

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:25.150
is public addressable on
the internet via HTTP.

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:28.120
In addition, it supports a
one-time transfer or a repeated

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.830
sync and is also available
in all regions where

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.329
GCS operates today.

00:06:34.329 --> 00:06:36.370
So let's do a little bit
of a walkthrough of what

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:39.265
this service looks like if
you actually went to use it.

00:06:39.265 --> 00:06:41.880
So to find it, you would
go into the Cloud Console,

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.870
and under Google
Cloud Storage, there

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.929
would be an option
called Transfer.

00:06:45.929 --> 00:06:47.470
And if you clicked
on that, you would

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:49.330
see a summary of all the
transfers that are running

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.790
and are done, and you would
also have the opportunity

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:53.500
to start a transfer.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.900
To start a transfer-- basically,
it involves three steps.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:01.000
First, identify the data
that you want to move.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.760
Second, tell us where
you want to move it.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.040
And thirdly, tell us
when you want to move it.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:07.667
It's just that simple.

00:07:07.667 --> 00:07:09.250
So we'll walk through
an example here.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:11.412
First, identify the
data you want to move.

00:07:11.412 --> 00:07:13.120
So here, hopefully
you guys can see this.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.160
I'll point it out to you.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.910
You can pick an Amazon S3 bucket
and give us the credentials

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:22.450
that we need to list objects
and pull data from that bucket.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:26.150
And then you can tell us what
prefixes to include or exclude.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.600
And you can also use
S3 metadata to choose

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.190
what objects to pull over.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:33.100
For instance, basing your
pull on last-modified time.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.400
So if you want to only
pull objects from S3 that

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.720
have been modified in the
last week, you can do that.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.780
The second step is to figure
out where to move this data.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.680
And to do that, you can pick
essentially any GCS bucket.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:50.580
And we support all
GCS storage classes.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.655
Finally, you have to tell
us when you want to move it.

00:07:53.655 --> 00:07:55.870
You have basically
two options here.

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:58.170
One is to just
move it right now.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:01.480
Or you can schedule a daily
sync at a time of your choosing.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.410
So for instance, if you
have a workload on AWS

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.810
that writes new objects
to S3 every day,

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:10.110
you can schedule a transfer job
to run every night at midnight

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.800
to sync those over to GCS.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.140
So that's a little bit about the
Cloud Storage Transfer Service.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:21.220
Now, the next step, on
this larger graphic here,

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:26.370
is if you have a presence in
a colo or even on-premise data

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.260
center that has good
connectivity to the internet.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:32.320
In that case, we offer
a product called GSutil

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.630
that you can use to
push your data to GCS.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:38.740
Gsutil is a command line
utility that is specifically

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:41.200
designed to help you push
data from a data center

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:45.034
that you are operating into GCP.

00:08:45.034 --> 00:08:46.700
And because it's a
command line utility,

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.130
you can use it for
your manual workflows

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:50.870
or as part of a script.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.870
You can also run it in a
background process, where

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.800
it's constantly syncing files
and directories to a GCS

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.480
bucket of your choosing.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:02.980
Now in addition to the sync and
copy options we talked about,

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.730
Gsutil also operates
in a streaming mode.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.810
So if you have a process that's
long running and outputs data

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:11.990
periodically, you can pipe
that output to Gsutil,

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:13.670
and it will push
it along to GCS.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:17.700
Gsutil has a lot of options.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:18.680
I'm not going to go
through all of them,

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:20.030
but I'll highlight a couple.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.910
It automatically retries if it
encounters ephemeral network

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:24.950
errors or errors from GCS.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:27.020
It also supports customer
managed and customer

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:28.730
supplied encryption keys.

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:31.400
And finally, it obeys
the object versioning

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:33.720
semantics that are typical
of most GCS clients.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.450
So let's talk a little bit
about some of the questions

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:41.010
that come up when
people use Gsutil.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.875
The first one is how do
I move my data quickly?

00:09:43.875 --> 00:09:45.750
So there are a couple
of features that Gsutil

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:47.920
has to help you do this.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.280
The first is using
multi-threading.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:55.490
So you can use the dash m
flag to ensure that the Gsutil

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.750
process is running
multiple threads which

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.670
are pulling all of
your files in parallel

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:02.190
and pushing them to GCS.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:04.620
Now, if that's not fast enough
to fill up your bandwidth

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:09.000
to GCP, you can also
split up and run

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.280
Gsutil on multiple hosts.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:14.400
And by doing so, move
your data in parallel

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.800
across multiple
Gsutil processes,

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:18.490
and use up more
of your bandwidth.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:20.370
And to do that, one
important thing to note

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:23.400
is that you'll have to manually
partition your data in advance

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.704
so that none of these
separate Gsutil processes

00:10:27.704 --> 00:10:28.870
are stomping on one another.

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.759
Now let's say you did that.

00:10:33.759 --> 00:10:35.800
One of the problems you
may encounter, especially

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:38.110
if you're moving a
large set of files,

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:41.840
is that there's one file,
and one Gsutil thread,

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:43.030
and one process moving it.

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:46.039
So if it were a really large
file, like a 100 gigabyte file,

00:10:46.039 --> 00:10:47.830
you might sit there
waiting for that thread

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.470
to move byte by byte for
that really large file,

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:52.026
and it just takes too long.

00:10:52.026 --> 00:10:54.670
So to address that
situation, Gsutil

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:57.430
has a feature called
Parallel Composite Uploads.

00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:00.250
What it does is it
basically takes large files,

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:02.800
breaks them into chunks, and
moves all of those chunks

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:04.900
simultaneously to GCS.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:06.910
Once they arrive at
GCS, those chunks

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:11.077
are now composited into
a single GCS object.

00:11:11.077 --> 00:11:12.660
It does require a
little bit of setup.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:14.410
You have to set some
environment variables

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:18.660
and compile a library for crcmod
before the host for Gsutil

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.160
is installed.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.050
In addition, it is
important to note

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:25.530
that if you are using certain
storage classes for GCS,

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:29.009
it does incur additional cost
as these composite pieces are

00:11:29.009 --> 00:11:29.550
put together.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.620
So a couple of other
assorted questions

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:37.260
that come up when
people are using Gsutil.

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:40.570
The first is, how do I control
how much bandwidth it's using?

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:43.680
For instance, if I already have
workloads in my data center,

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.260
I don't want Gsutil to
use up all the bandwidth

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.040
that those workloads
need to use.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:49.720
So how do I stop that?

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:51.750
Unfortunately, today,
Gsutil does not

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:54.450
have native support
for bandwidth throttle.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:59.760
But you can use tools like
Trickle or your network

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.710
administration
controls to control

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:04.800
how much bandwidth Gsutil uses.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.180
Another question
that comes up is

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.220
how do I preserve my
metadata, especially

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:12.000
if I'm trying to use
GCS or Gsutil to make

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.430
a backup of my data onto GCS?

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:18.760
To do that, you can use the dash
p option, which takes your file

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:20.640
metadata, your
POSIX file metadata,

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:23.490
and puts it into the
metadata of the GCS objects

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.680
as you copy them over.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.240
Finally, some
folks are concerned

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:28.710
about overwriting
data that already

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:30.702
exists in the GCS bucket.

00:12:30.702 --> 00:12:32.160
If you're concerned
about that, you

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.490
can use the dash n option, which
ensures that you do not clobber

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:38.590
any data that already exists.

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:41.130
So these are a couple of
little tools and tricks

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:42.450
that you can use with Gsutil.

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.170
There's actually a lot
more to read and understand

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:46.920
before you become
an expert on this.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:48.942
So before I hand it
back to Ben, I just

00:12:48.942 --> 00:12:51.400
want to highlight three things
that I covered off on today.

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:53.880
One, whenever you are
doing a large transfer,

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:55.740
think about how much
data you need to move

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:57.736
and how much bandwidth
you have to move it.

00:12:57.736 --> 00:12:59.610
Secondly, take a look
at some of the features

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.550
we walked through today and some
of our extensive documentation

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:05.182
on these two products
to figure out

00:13:05.182 --> 00:13:07.140
which features make the
most sense for the data

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:08.520
you're trying to move.

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:11.250
And thirdly, and most
importantly, in my opinion,

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.230
whenever you're doing
a large transfer,

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.930
always start with a
small sample of the data

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:18.120
that you intend to
move so you understand

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:20.010
what features make
sense for you,

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:23.040
how much it's going to cost,
and how long it's going to take.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:24.780
With that, let me
hand it back to Ben

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:27.684
to talk about the furthest
end of this spectrum,

00:13:27.684 --> 00:13:29.100
when you have a
remote data center

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:30.900
with limited bandwidth
or no connection

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:31.858
to the internet at all.

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:37.120
BENEDICT CHONG: Thanks, Ash.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:40.660
All right, so what if
you are in a situation

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:42.780
where you have very
little bandwidth,

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:44.530
or even if you have
bandwidth, a lot of it

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:48.880
is taken up by other
workloads, or you're

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:53.880
in a situation where
there's no network at all.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:58.390
So this is what we
call offline transfers.

00:13:58.390 --> 00:14:00.980
So for that, we have
Google Transfer Appliance.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:04.475
This is the first
hardware product

00:14:04.475 --> 00:14:07.830
that Google Cloud is shipping.

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:11.700
How many of you have
been to Moscone West

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:14.500
and seen it in person?

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:16.480
Just less than a handful.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:19.430
So we really have a real
appliance out in Moscone West,

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:22.594
on the showcase floor, that
you can actually touch.

00:14:22.594 --> 00:14:23.510
It's a real appliance.

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:26.880
It has all 60 drives in it.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:29.790
So this is a high
capacity storage server

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.230
that we ship to customers.

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:33.150
And the idea is that
you will lower your data

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:34.774
onto the appliance,
ship it back to us.

00:14:34.774 --> 00:14:37.410
We upload it for you.

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:41.160
I want to focus on the use
cases because there is--

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:43.540
when we talk to customers,
a lot of customers

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:45.630
start thinking about
interconnect and so on and so

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:49.290
forth, or upgrading
your network bandwidth.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:52.360
And we know that that's
a complicated process.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:55.470
There's the budget,
there is availability,

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:56.560
and so on and so forth.

00:14:56.560 --> 00:15:00.104
A lot of ISPs want you to
commit to a two-year period.

00:15:00.104 --> 00:15:01.770
And then your CIO
comes to you and says,

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:04.420
hey, I want to try out this
machine learning stuff,

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:08.890
but I do not want to invest
in high bandwidth networks.

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:10.320
So what do you do?

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:13.920
We have had relatively
good customer success,

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:15.960
where we ship them
a TA 100, which

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.450
is the 100 terabyte appliance.

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:21.190
They load it with 40 to
70 terabytes of data,

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:22.770
ship it back to us.

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:24.360
And then with that
amount of data,

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:28.290
they can actually run
a pretty good machine

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:30.300
learning modeling exercise.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:31.980
And after that,
they're happy enough

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.930
that they signed on with
us for the long term.

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:37.170
So this is really good for POCs.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.480
You are not blocked
by the availability

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.150
of network interconnect, all
that kind of conversations

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:44.380
with the ISPs.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:46.660
Another use case
is where customers

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:52.080
decide that they want to end
of life their on-prem storage.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:54.382
And then they want to move
all their data to Cloud.

00:15:54.382 --> 00:15:55.590
This is really good for them.

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:57.290
So we ship them this--

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:01.440
the big one that you see there,
load it with all the data,

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:02.400
ship it back to us.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:06.570
And we throw in GCS, generally
[INAUDIBLE] for the long term.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:11.190
How many of you
have seen our blog

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:15.330
where we talk about the
Schmidt Ocean Institute?

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:20.180
So believe it or not, there
are data centers on ships.

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:21.860
So this customer of ours--

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:24.620
we go on a cruise for
about a month or so.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:26.340
The ship takes the
smaller appliance.

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:27.849
And they rack it in the ship.

00:16:27.849 --> 00:16:29.640
There's actually a data
center in the ship.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:32.364
They collect all the data.

00:16:32.364 --> 00:16:33.780
And while they're
collecting data,

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:35.570
they're singing
into the appliance.

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:38.042
So once they reach port,
they ship it back to us.

00:16:38.042 --> 00:16:39.750
And then we load it
to GCS, and then they

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:43.830
share it with the rest of
the researchers worlwide.

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:45.660
And then we have
customers-- and this

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:49.140
is the last use case for
monthly long batches of data.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:53.880
So we have customers
with also poor networks,

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:57.330
or insufficient networks,
and they use this for it.

00:16:57.330 --> 00:17:00.020
Now, Transfer Appliance is
available in the US today.

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:01.380
But we're generally available.

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:03.660
We announced it
about a month ago.

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:07.530
We are starting a pilot program
in the EU, European Union,

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:08.880
for you.

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:13.560
So how many European
customers are there here?

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:14.460
All right, cool.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.869
So feel free to reach out to me.

00:17:16.869 --> 00:17:18.960
Or you can sign up
with the link here.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:21.569
We'll reach back out to
you and get you started,

00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:23.339
once we kick off that process.

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:26.980
And it's going to be
in a couple of weeks.

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:27.480
All right.

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:30.210
So quick summary of
the onboarding process.

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:31.630
You go to Pantheon--

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:35.400
sorry, Cloud Console for you
guys, request an appliance.

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:36.420
We send it to you.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:39.090
You configure it, set it
up, fill it with data,

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:40.560
ship it back to us.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:44.920
We do the painful stuff
of uploading data for you.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:48.030
And then the last step,
you decrypt your data

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:50.490
and move it to the location,
or the storage class,

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:54.640
that you want your data to
reside in the long term.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:57.600
And then the Appliance-- after
the data has been uploaded,

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.750
we wipe it securely.

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.960
Common questions include,
what do you do beyond wiping?

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:06.420
We wipe, we repartition
the hard drives,

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:09.390
reformat the hard
drives, reinstall the OS,

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:12.240
in case our customers decide to
install some application on it.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:14.490
Because we have heard, oh,
we are running Linux on it.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:16.690
OK, I'm going to
install some tools.

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:17.700
But no, no, no.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:20.667
So we reinstall the OS,
reinstall the application,

00:18:20.667 --> 00:18:22.500
and then get it ready
for the next customer.

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:24.060
So essentially,
the next customer

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:28.650
will get a new appliance.

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:31.950
All right, so I'm going to
walk through what Ash did,

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:34.750
the overall
Appliance experience.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:37.350
There, you can see
in Cloud Console,

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:39.120
if you go to the
Storage tab, there's

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:41.310
option now for
Transfer Appliance.

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:44.910
Fill out the form here.

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:47.610
It's good to know how much
data you want to move.

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:50.310
And please don't
submit multiple times,

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:52.270
because we process all
these things manually.

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:54.030
So it's kind of fun
to see you, like, some

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:56.800
customers-- if I hit
Submit more often,

00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:58.640
we'll get more appliances.

00:18:58.640 --> 00:18:59.480
We'll work with you.

00:18:59.480 --> 00:19:01.400
There's actually a person
at the end of this.

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:05.090
So we will work with you to
ship you the right appliances.

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:08.261
And again, there's
another person--

00:19:08.261 --> 00:19:10.010
there's another person
at the end of this,

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:11.390
so we will work with you.

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:14.670
So it's not a machine that
is going to shut you down

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:16.520
and [INAUDIBLE],,
these kind of things.

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:18.270
There's a real person,
and we'll make sure

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:21.972
that you get the best
support that you need.

00:19:21.972 --> 00:19:23.930
This is a picture of the
back of the appliance.

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:27.579
And you can see the real
thing in Moscone West.

00:19:27.579 --> 00:19:29.620
You can see there, it's
the standard power supply

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:30.380
connectors.

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:33.140
We have fiber optic ports here.

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:35.430
For them, we will
supply the transceivers,

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:37.330
should you need transceivers.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:39.630
There are four copper
internet ports.

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:41.420
Now why do we have four?

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:44.270
This allows you to do
network aggregation.

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:48.430
So you can run at a throughput
of 40 gigs per second

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:50.870
of throughput so that
networking itself

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:53.660
is no longer the
limiting factor for you

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:55.880
to capture data
onto the appliance.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:58.460
We have customers where the
internal network is only one

00:19:58.460 --> 00:19:59.060
gig.

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:01.140
By running it at
four at a time, we

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:03.500
are able to achieve four
gigabits per second,

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:05.140
which is not too bad.

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:07.140
It allows them to capture
data quickly enough.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.900
This is where we talk
about encryption.

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:18.940
So the data is
encrypted in transit.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:20.850
We use AES 256.

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:23.160
There's no phone home at all.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:28.010
The appliance is completely
disconnected from Google Cloud.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.910
The key is generated
by you guys.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:36.020
So as part of this setup,
you'll create a passphrase that

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:37.605
is used to generate the key.

00:20:37.605 --> 00:20:41.100
The encryption key is stored
in RAM on the appliance.

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:44.070
So while you are capturing
data, the key is being used.

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:46.740
Once you power off the appliance
and ship it back to us,

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:49.350
the key is gone.

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:52.770
When you-- what
we call rehydrate,

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:54.960
which is kind of like
decrypting the data--

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:56.675
that is done within
your project.

00:20:59.249 --> 00:21:00.540
And we don't have access to it.

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:05.370
So we provide a web-based UI.

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:08.020
You connect to the appliance
using a web browser.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:09.950
Today, we support Chrome.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:12.570
Don't ask me why.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:15.390
And you can see the job
status, the available physical

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.130
capacity, as well
as network usage.

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:20.160
So you know something
is happening there.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:22.360
We offer a few capture options.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:25.260
So the first two options, Linux
capture utility and Windows

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:27.810
capture utility,
for situations where

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:30.930
you need to do a fair
amount of authentication

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:34.770
to access your data
sources, right?

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:37.410
In that situation, you will
have a new Windows workstation

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:40.350
or Linux workstation sitting
between your data source

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:42.000
and the transfer appliance.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:45.180
And the capture utility will
read data from the data source

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:49.070
and push it to the
transfer appliance.

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:53.220
Mount NFSJ is essentially where
the appliance is able to mount

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:57.330
or where you allow the appliance
to mount your data sources.

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:00.570
And you'll pull the data
from the data source.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:03.960
And the last option there,
which is new and pretty popular

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:07.290
because it is an easy concept,
this is where the appliance

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:11.220
itself can be configured
to be a Nance box, right?

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:15.690
So you can mount it through
NFS and just copy or sync data

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:16.510
to it.

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:19.358
And it's a very easy
thing to do there.

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.930
So again, this is
a screenshot that

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:29.100
shows a couple of
options in progress.

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:31.190
So the first option there,
you can see NFS clients

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:33.320
where the appliance is
acting as an NFS client

00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:35.390
reading data from a NS box.

00:22:35.390 --> 00:22:38.790
And second option there is
that we have exposed part

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:40.260
of the appliance as an NFShe.

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:44.670
All right.

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:47.010
So once you have captured
data onto the appliance,

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:48.457
you ship it to us.

00:22:48.457 --> 00:22:50.790
This is actually the box that
you'll see in Moscone West

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:54.790
and we uploaded to
a Google bucket.

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:56.460
So the final step
is rehydration.

00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:03.150
That's where you will run a
VM, a GCVM in your GCP project.

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:05.580
This way, you would
decrypt the data

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:10.672
and move it to the GCP
bucket of your choice

00:23:10.672 --> 00:23:11.880
in the region of your choice.

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:15.220
So we don't control
where you move your data.

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:17.150
For example, you can decide to--

00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:18.630
you have data in the US.

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:21.520
You want to move it
to a European bucket.

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:25.570
You've been to run
the appliance process.

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:28.230
And then when you
decrypt the data,

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:32.980
it can be stored into
the GCS bucket in Europe.

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:37.110
And this is a screenshot of
the UI with the rehydration

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:37.770
jobs running.

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:42.520
And finally, when
all is said and done,

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:45.120
we provide a summary
dialog that tells you

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:46.860
how much data was transferred.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:49.620
And about files, we'll
highlight failures like,

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:52.080
you know, sometimes files are
missed because either they

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:54.650
are deleted or being
captured or there

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:56.400
are some integrity failures.

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:58.740
Integrity is where
we do a check sum.

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:02.265
So the source file
destination for we do a CR 32,

00:24:02.265 --> 00:24:04.140
and we'll tell you if
a bit has been flipped.

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:09.420
So we are working with
a bunch of partners

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:10.920
for transfer appliance.

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:13.350
So here is one use
case where you're

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:16.320
using Commvault to back
up your data, right?

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:18.660
But the first initial
copies of backup data

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:21.810
can be so large that it's
not feasible to transfer it

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:22.920
over the network.

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:26.190
So you use a transfer appliance
as a backup target, backup

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:27.480
to transfer appliance.

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:31.830
We upload that initial backup
[INAUDIBLE] into a GCS bucket,

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:35.490
and then you will do the
deltas over the network.

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:38.040
So we're working with another
